"DeepResearchGuard: Deep Research with Open-Domain Evaluation and Multi-Stage Guardrails for Safety (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10994v1)\nciteturn2700view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10994v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 271\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†Deep Research Frameworks. L19:     2. cite9†Evaluation for Deep Research. L20:     3. cite10†LLM, Agent, and Deep Research Safety. L21:   4. cite11†3 DeepResearchGuard Framework L22:     1. cite12†3.1 Taxonomy for Stages In DeepResearchGuard L23:     2. cite13†3.2 General Guard Rule for Agent in each Stage L24:     3. cite14†3.3 Memory Retrieval and Planning of Guard Agent L25:     4. cite15†3.4 Human intervention L26:     5. cite16†3.5 Guard Agents L27:       1. cite17†Input Guard Agent. L28:       2. cite18†Plan Guard Agent. L29:       3. cite19†Research Guard Agent. L30:       4. cite20†Output Guard Agent. L31:   5. cite21†4 DRSafeBench L32:   6. cite22†5 Experiment L33:     1. cite23†5.1 Experiment Setup L34:     2. cite24†5.2 Evaluation Metrics L35:     3. cite25†5.3 Baseline vs. Guarded Pipelines with DeepResearchGuard L36:       1. cite26†DeepResearchGuard substantially boosts DSR across models while keeping ORR low. L37:       2. cite27†Integrating DeepResearchGuard consistently lifts report quality across all models. L38: cite28†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L39: \nL40: arXiv:2510.10994v1 [cs.CL] 13 Oct 2025\nL41: # DeepResearchGuard: Deep Research with Open-Domain Evaluation and Multi-Stage Guardrails for Safety\nL42: Wei-Chieh Huang Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Henry Peng Zou Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Yaozu Wu Affiliation: University of Tokyo    Dongyuan Li Affiliation: University of Tokyo    Yankai Chen Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Weizhi Zhang Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Yangning Li Affiliation: Tsinghua University    Angelo Zangari Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Jizhou Guo Affiliation: Shanghai Jiao Tong University{whuang80, ychen588, psyu}@uic.edu, jiangrh@csis.u-tokyo.ac.jp    Chunyu Miao Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Liancheng Fang Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Langzhou He Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago    Renhe Jiang Affiliation: University of Tokyo    Philip S.\nL43: Yu Affiliation: University of Illinois Chicago\nL44: ###### Abstract\nL45: Deep research frameworks have shown promising capabilities in synthesizing comprehensive reports from web sources. While deep research possesses significant potential to address complex issues through planning and research cycles, existing frameworks are deficient in sufficient evaluation procedures and stage-specific protections.\nL46: They typically treat evaluation as exact match accuracy of question-answering, but overlook crucial aspects of report quality such as credibility, coherence, breadth, depth, and safety. This oversight may result in hazardous or malicious sources being integrated into the final report. To address these issues, we introduce DeepResearchGuard, a comprehensive framework featuring four-stage safeguards with open-domain evaluation of references and reports.\nL47: We assess performance across multiple metrics, e.g., defense success rate and over-refusal rate, and five key report dimensions. In the absence of a suitable safety benchmark, we introduce DRSafeBench, a stage-wise benchmark for deep research safety. Our evaluation spans diverse state-of-the-art LLMs, including GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5-flash, DeepSeek-v3, and o4-mini. DeepResearchGuard achieves an average defense success rate improvement of 18.16% while reducing over-refusal rate by 6%.\nL48: The input guard provides the most substantial early-stage protection by filtering out obvious risks, while the plan and research guards enhance citation discipline and source credibility. Through extensive experiments, we show that DeepResearchGuard enables comprehensive open-domain evaluation and stage-aware defenses that effectively block harmful content propagation, while systematically improving report quality without excessive over-refusal rates.\nL49: The code can be found via cite29†https://github.com/Jasonya/DeepResearchGuard†github.com .\nL50: ^{†}^{†}footnotetext: ^{†}Corresponding author.\nL51: ## 1 Introduction\nL52: Extensive literature research, technical assessments with thorough reports, or complex problem-solving typically require days or even weeks of diligent labor from specialists to accomplish. The process of investigating sources, comparing findings, and arranging them into a coherent narrative is inherently time-consuming.\nL53: To address this, “deep research agents” (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-03160; DBLP:conf/acl/WuZLXJ25), built on state-of-the-art Large Language Models (LLMs) (zou2025testnuc; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-00753; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-16804), take open-ended user queries, decompose them into sub-tasks, iteratively search literature, and draft structured reports in response to the queries.\nL54: By compressing multi-day research cycles to minutes, deep research frameworks (xu2025comprehensive; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-05495; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-09477) offer a promising solution for augmenting human expertise (miao2025recode; zou2025call) and facilitating domain knowledge fusion in producing comprehensive reports.\nL55: However, delivering such high-quality outputs at this speed hinges on integrating high factual correctness with comprehensive coverage, which in turn foregrounds the critical challenge of rigorous evaluation (li2025reportbenchevaluatingdeepresearch).\nL56: Despite the variety of output formats in deep research, the majority of current evaluation efforts focus on question-answering (QA) tasks (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2506-20670; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-16834). Conventional QA standards (rein2023gpqa) rely on lexical correspondence between generated responses and a limited collection of reference answers (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-17005; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-04588).\nL57: However, this evaluation paradigm is limited to assessing the exact match accuracy of final answers while overlooking critical dimensions of report quality, including credibility, coherence, safety, depth, and breadth. Moreover, open-domain research represents a more complex use case than QA tasks (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2506-18959), requiring comprehensive evaluation of the entire research process and output quality.\nL58: Consequently, the existing QA benchmarks are inadequate for evaluating deep research frameworks, and the evaluation methodologies remain poorly defined.\nL59: cite30†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of DeepResearchGuard. It operationalizes deep research via four guarded stages. The Input and Output Guard Agent share a taxonomy with severity-based interventions. The Plan Guard Agent validates plan safety and the decomposition quality issues. The Reference Guard Agent screens references and scores resources on helpfulness, reliability, and timeliness. The final report is assessed in terms of credibility, coherence, safety, depth, and breadth.\nL60: The guard report is produced concurrently with the final report to users. At all stages, when agent’s confidence falls below a predefined threshold, a human reviewer can accept, override, or relabel the decision.\nL61: Open-domain deep research (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2506-18096; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-19253) rarely admits a single ground-truth answer, necessitating evaluation of the complete multi-stage process rather than solely the final output. Yet many deep research systems operate as black boxes, obscuring intermediate decisions and preventing a systematic overview of their stage-by-stage operations.\nL62: While the cycle of planning, searching, and reflecting allows deep research frameworks to reconsolidate the understanding of the task, this multi-stage process also exacerbates issues: a harmful prompt, an unreliable plan, or a dubious reference can spread through subsequent iterations to contaminate downstream outputs. Minor inaccuracies can be magnified into substantial deviations as the agent revisits and expands upon its intermediate outputs.\nL63: Consequently, deep research frameworks expose a significantly larger attack surface than LLMs or agent interactions (belcak2025universal; chen2025embracing; luo2025large). Each stage, including input, planning, research, and output, presents a risk for adversaries to introduce malicious content without adequate safeguards.\nL64: Nevertheless, current research prioritizes performance optimization while largely disregarding security considerations in deep research framework design (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-03160; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-20201).\nL65: To address these issues, we present DeepResearchGuard: an open-domain evaluation framework with multi-stage guardrails that safeguard four stages of deep research—input, plan, research, and output. As shown in Figure cite31†1 , it evaluates prompt safety at the input stage, examines plan quality and associated risk at the plan stage, verifies resource credibility at the research stage, and assesses report quality and user-intent alignment at the output stage.\nL66: This stage-level guard architecture halts harmful content propagation before it compromises subsequent stages. Our design draws inspiration from expert review practices: domain specialists assess sources based on institutional authority, currency, and claim validity, while evaluating reports for argumentative coherence, analytical depth and breadth, and evidential credibility.\nL67: To rigorously evaluate DeepResearchGuard, we introduce DRSafeBench, an 828-query benchmark that stress-tests the complete deep research workflow. The benchmark covers diverse open-domain topics and includes harmful inputs with adversarially generated benign queries. This enables precise measurement of whether DeepResearchGuard can effectively distinguish dangerous content from innocuous prompts that share similar linguistic patterns across all four stages. Our key contributions are summarized below:\nL68:   * •\nL69: \nL70: First multi-stage safeguard for deep research. To the best of our knowledge, DeepResearchGuard is the first work that designs and studies agent guardrails for deep research workflows, introducing stage-specific safeguards with memory mechanisms and human interventions.\nL71: \nL72:   * •\nL73: Comprehensive evaluation for open-domain deep research task. We propose an open-domain evaluation protocol that assesses both the references and the reports produced by deep research systems, rather than relying solely on QA-style exact-match metrics.\nL74: \nL75:   * •\nL76: Safety-focused benchmark: DRSafeBench. We introduce a systematically constructed benchmark containing adversarial queries designed to probe the defense success rate versus over-refusal rate trade-off, enabling comprehensive evaluation of baseline models and DeepResearchGuard-enhanced systems.\nL77: ## 2 Related Work\nL78: #### Deep Research Frameworks.\nL79: Some previous research (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-03160) has investigated LLMs or agents (huang2025madiave; zhang2025personaagent) for deep research (wei2025webagent), which execute complex tasks such as literature reviews (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-04183), multi-hop reasoning (deng2025atom), and report production. A common approach incorporates deconstructing the issue into multiple phases of planning, retrieval (li2025admtree), and synthesis for the report (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2506-17188).\nL80: Agent architectures employ iterative self-inquiry and external tool APIs to improve responses with corroborative evidence incrementally (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-19253).\nL81: However, the current work primarily focuses on enhancing workflow and improving search methods by reinforcement learning (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-03160; li2025reinforcementlearningfoundationsdeep) or integrating robust reasoning flow (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-21776), but prior studies neglect the potential risks associated with advanced functions, which may introduce greater hazards, specifically for the self-inquiry and accumulated deep research work.\nL82: #### Evaluation for Deep Research.\nL83: Evaluation on traditional open-domain QA (DBLP:conf/emnlp/Yang0ZBCSM18; DBLP:journals/tacl/TrivediBKS22) relies on exact-match for short answers, but recent evaluation focus more on multi-hop retrieval (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-14249) and long-form synthesis (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-17005; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-04588; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-16834).\nL84: Accordingly, evaluation has shifted to multi-document, multi-step benchmarks in open-domain settings (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-15391; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2402-14116; li2025reportbench; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-05495). Despite recent progress towards more comprehensive evaluation (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-06600), performance on reference and report remains uneven.\nL85: Many studies still rely on Wikipedia-like or curated corpora (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-09516), or domain specific metrics (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-05748). Motivated by how human experts appraise sources and reports, our framework integrates a stage-aware evaluation protocol that scores both references and final reports in deep research workflows to foster quality and safety assessment.\nL86: #### LLM, Agent, and Deep Research Safety.\nL87: Existing safeguards mostly target a single interaction surface: LLM-level moderation screens prompts and responses (DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-06674; DBLP:conf/acl/WenZM025) for harmful content or jailbreaks (DBLP:conf/nips/HanREJL00D24; DBLP:conf/iclr/YinLPZMVRAXW25).\nL88: Although multi-agent work extends protection to monitor tools (DBLP:conf/acl/WangZYWMGWW25; DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-04392; wu2025psg), it lacks systematic integration and operate in isolation rather than as a cohesive defense system (DBLP:conf/iclr/ZhangHMYWZWZ25; DBLP:conf/acl/LuoDL000X25).\nL89: In contrast, deep research unfolds over multiple stages (input, planning, research, report) and introduces distinct risks (xu2025comprehensive), such as plan drift and web-retrieval threats that bypass single-turn moderation. Hence, the guardrails designed for LLMs and single-turn agents are insufficient for deep research systems; they require stage-aware, interlocking safeguards that validate input, plans, vet sources, and verify report outputs across the entire pipeline.\nL90: ## 3 DeepResearchGuard Framework\nL91: ### 3.1 Taxonomy for Stages In DeepResearchGuard\nL92: The taxonomy and rules in DeepResearchGuard start from an initial examination, integration, and consolidation of prior research on input-output taxonomies for LLM Agents (DBLP:conf/iclr/YinLPZMVRAXW25; DBLP:conf/nips/HanREJL00D24; DBLP:conf/acl/WangZYWMGWW25). We review and adapt the most relevant aspects for deep research.\nL93: Furthermore, we formulate objective standards and stage-specific taxonomies for the planning, research, and output stages, establishing a unified framework design for deep research tasks. In our taxonomy (see Appendix ), we design the severity $s$ to quantify the harmful level of the content in each stage, as represented in Figure cite31†1 . If $s=3$, DeepResearchGuard terminates the process to prevent harmful content from propagating.\nL94: If $s\\in\\{1,2\\}$, DeepResearchGuard revises problematic content and passes the revised content to the subsequent stage.\nL95: ### 3.2 General Guard Rule for Agent in each Stage\nL96: DeepResearchGuard applies multiple guard agents in the deep research workflow at input, planning, research, and output stages, evaluates content with memory, and escalates to human review when confidence is low. Each guard agent classifies content, assigns a severity, and edits or rejects it. Let $k\\in\\{\\text{input},\\text{plan},\\text{output}\\}$ index the Input, Plan, and Output guards, respectively.\nL97: Each guard $G_{k}$ receives the upstream message $m_{k}$, the contextual state $C$, and the long-term memory $M$. It predicts a category $y_{k}\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ and a severity $s_{k}\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, then chooses an action $a_{k}$ and produces a revised message $m_{k}^{\\prime}$, if required:\nL98:  | $\\displaystyle(y_{k},s_{k})$  | $\\displaystyle=f_{k}(m_{k},C,M),$  |  | (1)\nL99:  | $\\displaystyle(a_{k},m_{k}^{\\prime})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\pi_{k}(y_{k},s_{k},m_{k}).$  |  | (2)\nL100: \nL101: Here, $f_{k}$ is the stage-$k$ LLM classifier and $\\pi_{k}$ is the stage-specific policy. If $s_{k}=3$, the pipeline gets hard refusal by DeepResearchGuard; if $s_{k}\\in\\{1,2\\}$, the policy either redacts and resumes or repairs and runs.\nL102: ### 3.3 Memory Retrieval and Planning of Guard Agent\nL103: We cache processed cases for retrieval to support evaluation. As shown in Figure cite31†1 , the first step of each guard agent is to look up a similar case in the memory.\nL104: Let $k\\in\\{\\text{input},\\text{plan},\\text{research},\\text{output}\\}$ denote the guard stage; $p$ the current query prompt; $\\{p_{j}\\}$ prior contents $j$ in long-term memory; $y_{j}\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ the stored category label for item $j$; $\\tau_{a,j}\\in[0,1]$ the stored classifier confidence; $s(p,p_{j})\\in[0,1]$ a semantic similarity; $\\tau_{\\text{sim}}$ the similarity threshold; and $L\\in\\mathbb{N}$ the number of top matches kept.\nL105: The process of finding similar cases in long-term memory and forming the prompt for the guard is described in Algorithm cite32†1 . In addition, the planning variable $\\mathit{approach}\\in\\{\\textsc{standard},\\textsc{cautious},\\textsc{conservative}\\}$ controls the human intervention threshold $\\tau_{h}$ and the reasoning budget $C_{r}$ (e.g., $\\tau_{h}{=}0.5/0.7/0.8$ with $C_{r}{=}\\textsc{medium}/\\textsc{high}$).\nL106: We also expose four stage-$k$ boolean risk flags used by planning: $X_{\\mathrm{ce}}$ (cross_stage_escalation: severities across recent stages are nondecreasing with at least one strict increase), $X_{\\mathrm{acc}}$ (accumulated_high_sev: the count of high-severity events in a fixed window occurs at least twice), $X_{\\mathrm{hum}}$ (human_intervened: a user/reviewer overrode, edited, or explicitly confirmed the decision at the current or immediately preceding step), and $X_{\\mathrm{vhr}}$ (very_high_risk_keywords: the input matches a curated high-risk lexicon or a classifier’s very high risk collections).\nL107: These combine into a single trigger $\\chi_{k}\\!=\\!X_{\\mathrm{ce}}\\lor X_{\\mathrm{acc}}\\lor X_{\\mathrm{hum}}\\lor X_{\\mathrm{vhr}}\\in\\{0,1\\}$: when $\\chi_{k}{=}1$, the planner selects a stricter mode (raising $\\tau_{h}$ and increasing $C_{r}$). If the similar content has high risk or a low confidence score in memory, or the previous stage has content with severity greater than 1, then the approach will be raised to cautious, as shown in Algorithm cite33†2 .\nL108: Algorithm 1 Memory Retrieval (Stage $k$)\nL109: \nL110: 1: Input: stage $k$; query $p$; stage-$k$ memory $\\mathcal{M}_{k}=\\{(p_{j},y_{j},\\tau_{a,j})\\}_{j=1}^{N_{k}}$; similarity $s(\\cdot,\\cdot)\\!\\in[0,1]$; threshold $\\tau_{\\text{sim}}$; top size $L$\nL111: \nL112: 2: Output: index set $\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)}$; short context $\\tilde{C}_{k}(p)$\nL113: \nL114: 3: $\\mathcal{J}_{k}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL115: \nL116: 4: for $j\\leftarrow 1$ to $N_{k}$ do\nL117: \nL118: 5:   $s_{j}\\leftarrow s(p,p_{j})$\nL119: \nL120: 6:   if $s_{j}>\\tau_{\\text{sim}}$ then\nL121: 7:    $\\mathcal{J}_{k}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{J}_{k}\\cup\\{(j,s_{j})\\}$\nL122: \nL123: 8:   end if\nL124: \nL125: 9: end for\nL126: \nL127: 10: Sort-Desc$(\\mathcal{J}_{k}$ by $s_{j})$\nL128: \nL129: 11: $\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)}\\leftarrow\\textsc{Head}(\\mathcal{J}_{k},L)$\nL130: \nL131: 12: $\\tilde{C}_{k}(p)\\leftarrow\\epsilon$\nL132: \nL133: 13: for each $(j,s_{j})\\in\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)}$ do\nL134: \nL135: 14:   $\\tilde{C}_{k}(p)\\leftarrow\\tilde{C}_{k}(p)\\ \\oplus\\ \\mathrm{format}\\!\\big(p,y_{j},\\tau_{a,j},s_{j}\\big)$\nL136: \nL137: 15: end for\nL138: \nL139: 16: return $\\big(\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)},\\tilde{C}_{k}(p)\\big)$\nL140: Algorithm 2 Approach Planning (Stage $k$)\nL141: \nL142: 1: Input: previous-stage severity $s_{\\mathrm{prev}}$; retrieved indices $\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)}$ with stored severities $\\{s_{j}\\}_{j\\in\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)}}$; booleans $X_{\\mathrm{ce}}$, $X_{\\mathrm{acc}}$, $X_{\\mathrm{hum}}$, $X_{\\mathrm{vhr}}$; flag low_confidence\nL143: \nL144: 2: Output: $\\mathit{approach}\\in\\{\\textsc{standard},\\textsc{cautious},\\textsc{conservative}\\}$; $\\tau_{h}$; $C_{r}$\nL145: 3: $\\mathit{approach}\\leftarrow\\textsc{standard}$; $\\tau_{h}\\leftarrow 0.5$; $C_{r}\\leftarrow\\textsc{medium}$\nL146: \nL147: 4: $has\\_high\\_sev\\leftarrow\\bigl(\\exists\\,j\\in\\mathcal{J}_{k}^{(L)}:\\ s_{j}\\geq 2\\bigr)$\nL148: \nL149: 5: $\\chi_{k}\\leftarrow X_{\\mathrm{ce}}\\lor X_{\\mathrm{acc}}\\lor X_{\\mathrm{hum}}\\lor X_{\\mathrm{vhr}}$\nL150: \nL151: 6: if $\\chi_{k}=1$ then\nL152: \nL153: 7:   $\\mathit{approach}\\leftarrow\\textsc{conservative}$; $\\tau_{h}\\leftarrow 0.8$; $C_{r}\\leftarrow\\textsc{high}$\nL154: 8: else if $(s_{\\mathrm{prev}}\\geq 2)\\ \\lor\\ has\\_high\\_sev\\ \\lor\\ \\textit{low\\_confidence}$ then\nL155: \nL156: 9:   $\\mathit{approach}\\leftarrow\\textsc{cautious}$; $\\tau_{h}\\leftarrow 0.7$; $C_{r}\\leftarrow\\textsc{medium}$\nL157: \nL158: 10: end if\nL159: \nL160: 11: return $(\\mathit{approach},\\ \\tau_{h},\\ C_{r})$\nL161: ### 3.4 Human intervention\nL162: \nL163: At stage $k$, the guard agent returns an evaluation and a confidence score $\\tau_{a}$. If $\\tau_{a}<\\tau_{h}$, DeepResearchGuard prompts the user to confirm the decision (see Appendix ), in human evaluation portion as shown in Figure cite31†1 . The user may accept, rewrite the content, mark as safe/unsafe, or view similar cases. This process can be formulated as:\nL164:  | $$(y_{k},s_{k})=\\begin{cases}\\big(y_{k}^{\\mathrm{user}},\\,s_{k}^{\\mathrm{user}}\\big),&\\tau_{a}<\\tau_{h},\\\\[4.0pt]\nL165: \\big(y_{k}^{\\mathrm{agent}},\\,s_{k}^{\\mathrm{agent}}\\big),&\\tau_{a}\\geq\\tau_{h}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (3)\nL166: ### 3.5 Guard Agents\nL167: #### Input Guard Agent.\nL168: As shown in the input guard portion of Figure cite31†1 , the input guard ingests the user query $m$, retrieves similar cases from long-term memory $M$ via Algorithm cite32†1 , and selects a planning approach $\\mathit{approach}$ for evaluation via Algorithm cite33†2 . It then assigns the query a category $y\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ and a severity level $s\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ via Eq. (cite34†1 ) and Eq. (cite35†2 ).\nL169: If $s=3$, DeepResearchGuard terminates the process; otherwise, for $s\\in\\{1,2\\}$, it invokes an LLM to refine the query conditioned on $y$. When the agent’s confidence $\\tau_{a}$ falls below the threshold $\\tau_{h}$, the final label follows the user-override rule in Eq. (cite36†3 ). Long-term memory persists the final evaluation and rationale, while short-term memory retains stage-wise results to pass to the subsequent guard at stage $k{+}1$. The prompt of the input guard agent can be found in Appendix .\nL170: #### Plan Guard Agent.\nL171: As shown in the plan guard portion in Figure cite31†1 , the plan guard ingests the research plan $m$, retrieves similar cases from long-term memory $M$ via Algorithm cite32†1 , and selects an evaluation approach $\\mathit{approach}$ via Algorithm cite33†2 . It then assigns a category $y\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ and severity $s\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ via Eq. (cite34†1 ) and Eq. (cite35†2 ).\nL172: If $s=3$, DeepResearchGuard halts execution or requests replanning; otherwise, for $s\\in\\{1,2\\}$, it edits $m$ to produce a safer, sufficient plan $m^{\\prime}$ based on $y$. When the agent’s confidence $\\tau_{a}$ falls below the threshold $\\tau_{h}$, the final decision follows the user-override rule in Eq. (cite36†3 ). Long-term memory persists the label, severity, confidence, and rationale; short-term memory retains the revised plan and metadata to inform the next stage.\nL173: The prompt of the plan guard agent can be found in Appendix .\nL174: #### Research Guard Agent.\nL175: \nL176: Given candidate references $\\mathcal{D}=\\{d_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N}$, for each $d\\in\\mathcal{D}$, DeepResearchGuard evaluates its URL, title, and content using two functions, is_URL_malicious and is_reference_malicious, which both return $1$ if the URL or the content is harmful; the safety indicator for $d$ is then defined as:\nL177: \nL178:  | $$f(d)=1-\\max\\big(\\texttt{is\\_URL\\_malicious}(d),\\ \\texttt{is\\_reference\\_malicious}(d)\\big)\\in\\{0,1\\}.$$  |  | (4)\nL179: In addition to the maliciousness check, DeepResearchGuard evaluates each reference by its helpfulness $s_{h}(r)$, authority $s_{a}(r)$, and timeliness $s_{t}(r)$, as shown in the reference guard portion in Figure cite31†1 . For a reference $r$, DeepResearchGuard generates scores $s_{h}(r),s_{a}(r),s_{t}(r)$ from 1 to 5 and computes the average score $S_{rc}(r)$. If $r$ is flagged as malicious, we override its score to the minimum $S(r)\\leftarrow S_{\\min}$ and tag it as malicious.\nL180: All references with their scores and malicious indicators are then passed to the reporter. When the agent’s confidence $\\tau_{a}$ falls below the threshold $\\tau_{h}$, the final decision follows the user-override rule in Eq. (cite36†3 ), for each reference $r$. The references are stored by reference in either short-term or long-term memory. The corresponding prompt can be found in Appendix .\nL181: #### Output Guard Agent.\nL182: The output guard ingests the drafted report $m$, retrieves similar cases from long-term memory $M$ via Algorithm cite32†1 , and selects an evaluation approach $\\mathit{approach}$ via Algorithm cite33†2 . It then assigns a category $y\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ and severity $s\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ via Eq. (cite34†1 ) and Eq. (cite35†2 ). If $s=3$, it blocks the report. For $s\\in\\{1,2\\}$, it transforms $m$ into a safe, policy-compliant output $m^{\\prime}$ by redacting unsafe content.\nL183: When $\\tau_{a}<\\tau_{h}$, the final decision follows the user override rule in Eq. (cite36†3 ). Then, the output guard then evaluates the report along with five dimensions (coherence, credibility, safety, depth, and breadth), as shown in output guard portion in Figure cite31†1 . This produces per-dimension scores $s_{\\mathrm{coh}}(r),\\,s_{\\mathrm{cred}}(r),\\,s_{\\mathrm{safe}}(r),\\,s_{\\mathrm{dep}}(r),\\,s_{\\mathrm{br}}(r)$, accordingly.\nL184: The overall score is $S_{\\mathrm{out}}(r)=\\mathbf{w}^{\\top}\\mathbf{s}\\cdot{\\mathrm{out}}(r)$. These results are persisted in long-term memory, and a consolidated guard report is generated and presented to users. The prompt can be found in Appendix .\nL185: ## 4 DRSafeBench\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDUAL-Bench: Measuring Over-Refusal and Robustnessin Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.10846v1)\nciteturn2700view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 